WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.200
Why is the Google Pixel growing,&nbsp;
while everyone else is dying?

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.800
Besides Apple, all other Android&nbsp;
brands are losing users… except Google.&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.560
And the Google Pixel has been growing a lot too.

00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:18.160
But it gets even weirder.
Global smartphone shipments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:24.240
are also forecast to fall 12.9% in 2026.
The biggest decline in a decade.&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.840
Android shipments are expected to drop 15%.
But not Google Pixel.&nbsp;

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.640
It's projected to grow 19%.
Something big is happening.&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:45.360
The entire smartphone industry is changing, and&nbsp;
the one winning seems to be the Google Pixel.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.040
Things have changed a lot.
Back in 2015, Google didn't even make smartphones.&nbsp;

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:56.240
But around that time, two people at Google sat&nbsp;
down for a meeting that would change everything.&nbsp;

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.240
And since then, the Pixel has&nbsp;
slowly been taking ground.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.680
Since its launch in 2016 and 2023,&nbsp;
Google shipped 37 million Pixel phones.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.960
And that momentum's only continuing.
25% growth in 2025.&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.360
13% projected growth for the Pixel in 2026.
Then, that projection jumped to 18.9%.&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.560
But this gets even stranger&nbsp;
when we take a step back.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:25.120
In 2018, global smartphone&nbsp;
sales peaked at 1.55 billion.&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.720
Then began to decline.
After a brief rebound, things are looking rough.&nbsp;

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:37.040
Shipments are declining 12.9% in&nbsp;
2026. That's an unbelievable drop.&nbsp;

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.040
Plus, any rebounding growth&nbsp;
afterwards is pretty small.&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:45.600
But strangely enough, the ones&nbsp;
struggling the most are cheap Android.&nbsp;

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.200
The smaller brands.
[Nabila Popal, Senior Research Director, IDC]: "We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:52.000
expect consolidation as smaller players&nbsp;
exit, and low-end vendors face sharp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.913
shipment declines amid supply constraints&nbsp;
and lower demand at higher price points."&nbsp;

00:01:56.913 --> 00:01:59.600
[Nabila Popal, Senior Research Director, IDC]:&nbsp;
"Although shipments will witness a record drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:07.360
smartphone ASP [average selling price] is&nbsp;
projected to rise 14% to a record $523 this year."&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:13.200
Analysts say cheap, sub-$100 smartphones&nbsp;
might become *"permanently uneconomical."*&nbsp;

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.880
Not "unviable until things&nbsp;
blow over." Permanently.&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:23.520
Between 2016 and 2025, the number of&nbsp;
smartphone owners increased by around 96%.

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:26.640
But that's slowing down. A lot.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.640
81.6% of the US population has a smartphone.&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.040
You'd think the US, with its strong buying&nbsp;
power, but also being mostly saturated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:39.920
would mean it's time for phones with lower prices&nbsp;
and strong value to be doing well globally.&nbsp;

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:43.520
In countries that don't have the&nbsp;
same level of smartphone penetration.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.680
To catch first-time smartphone buyers&nbsp;
in markets with less purchasing power.&nbsp;

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.720
This is what many brands have been trying to do.&nbsp;

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.040
But that's not what's happening.
Global, overall smartphone shipments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:02.080
are shrinking. But premium smartphone sales —&nbsp;
those costing over $600 — are broadly growing.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:09.120
And the one growing the fastest is Google.
105% year over year. Doubling in sales.&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:14.720
Google climbed to 4th place in the US,&nbsp;
overtaking TCL, which shrunk by 23%.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.880
It's also taken 10% market share in Japan, which&nbsp;
is one of the most iPhone-saturated markets.&nbsp;

00:03:20.880 --> 00:03:24.480
So is it just cheap brands&nbsp;
shrinking and premium growing?&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.560
Well, this is true, but the story&nbsp;
seems to be more complicated.

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:32.880
Google passed Samsung in Japan to&nbsp;
take the #2 spot in early 2026.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:39.920
And that was in large part thanks to the Pixel&nbsp;
9a, a phone at $499 and much cheaper than the 9.&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:46.640
Morgan Stanley reported that "Apple's net&nbsp;
switching rate improved to 11%, a 5-year high. All&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.920
other major smartphone brands, with the exception&nbsp;
of Google, exhibit negative net switching rates."

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.440
All these brands are now experiencing&nbsp;
negative switching rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.360
except Apple, and of course, Google.
[Ben Schoon, 9to5Google]: "In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:04.320
outside of customers upgrading within their&nbsp;
respective ecosystems, Google Pixel and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:10.880
iPhone are the only brands gaining new users."
Android shipments are expected to drop 15%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.760
with many people switching to iPhone.
But the Pixel is the exception.&nbsp;

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.840
The phone market as a whole is shrinking, Androids&nbsp;
are shrinking — what is Google doing differently?&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:27.600
And what's causing cheaper phones to die?
To find out, we need to go back to 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:37.360
when Google made a decision&nbsp;
that would change everything.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.560
Despite how advanced technology is, we still plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.520
write, and communicate the same&nbsp;
way as decades ago: Typing.&nbsp;

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:51.600
Manually typing, everything. But we&nbsp;
all know that speaking is way faster.&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:57.040
It’s easier to reply to messages, write mental&nbsp;
notes, and plan things without losing your train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:04.480
of thought. But… we still type, even though&nbsp;
all our devices have microphones, ready to go.&nbsp;

