00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:54.000
In 2016, Google CEO Sundar Pichai and&nbsp;
hardware chief Rick Osterloh formed a plan.

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.360
Currently, the Pixel was using the Qualcomm&nbsp;
Snapdragon, like most other Androids.&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:05.200
But it seemed like Google wasn't really pushing&nbsp;
this thing. It almost seemed like a prototype.&nbsp;

00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:11.200
That's because Google wanted the Pixel using&nbsp;
its very own SoC chip — the Google Tensor.&nbsp;

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:14.703
But this would take time.
A long time.&nbsp;

00:07:14.703 --> 00:07:17.760
[Sundar Pichai]: "Part of the reason that I&nbsp;
think the team has been more modest in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:23.360
approach with Pixel over the past 18 months or&nbsp;
so is because they've been waiting for Tensor."

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.880
A lot of Androids feel like a different wrapper&nbsp;
around the same thing. Just a different camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:32.720
price, aesthetic. Things&nbsp;
that can be easily copied.&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:38.000
So if you really want to stand out, offer&nbsp;
things that are very hard to replicate.&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:41.280
Apple has been doing this&nbsp;
with Apple Silicon for years.&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:46.080
In 2017, Google assembled a team to&nbsp;
work on the chip — 76 semiconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:49.200
researchers who specialised in&nbsp;
AI and ML, machine learning.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:53.520
You might roll your eyes at&nbsp;
this. "Another AI phone, cool."&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:08:00.240
But this was way before the AI boom.
ChatGPT didn't release until late 2022.&nbsp;

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:06.240
Not only was Google investing in their own unique&nbsp;
chip, but they were betting on the far future.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.320
Google bet that AI would not only be huge, but&nbsp;
that it would move from the cloud to the device.&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:18.240
And that's exactly what's happening right now.
But back in 2017, this would take a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.440
lot of work and time.
Plus, the current Pixel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.680
models weren't that popular.
Sales were even declining.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:32.880
But eventually, came the Pixel 6 in 2021. The&nbsp;
official Google flagship, featuring the Tensor.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:38.480
Google was very early to this game, and it still&nbsp;
had to compete on all the other benchmarks.&nbsp;

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:43.600
And at a $599 or even $899 price point.
Not cheap.&nbsp;

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.080
Plus, the specs and speed weren't that impressive.&nbsp;

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:51.360
That, and it was having thermal&nbsp;
problems, like overheating.&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.240
In fact, it was the #1 reason&nbsp;
for customers returning Pixels.&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.400
But it was offering some big&nbsp;
things other Androids lacked.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:04.880
It had Call Screen, which uses Google&nbsp;
Assistant to answer calls from unknown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:09.040
contacts, and would even ask them who&nbsp;
they were and the reason for the call.&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:13.040
You can then see the transcript&nbsp;
and decide to answer or hang up.&nbsp;

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.680
There's "Hold For Me," where Google Assistant&nbsp;
would literally sit on hold and then notify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.120
you when you can talk to someone.
There's voice message transcription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.560
estimated wait times, AI to detect scams,&nbsp;
plus a long list of photo features.&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:32.160
The Pixel had a lot going for it — plus,&nbsp;
surprisingly, a lack of bloatware, which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.160 --> 00:09:36.480
one thing that annoys me about Samsung.
A lot of differences between Androids&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:39.520
feel pretty small.
But not the Pixel.&nbsp;

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.280
"I have absolutely no spam calls compared to&nbsp;
several a day before! I like the Hold For Me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:50.160
option too! No more BS car warranty&nbsp;
spam and it will block texts too!"&nbsp;

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:54.320
"I have used the Call Screening feature so&nbsp;
much since I got this phone. Between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:59.920
spam detection and call screening, I haven't&nbsp;
picked up a spam caller on this phone at all."&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:04.720
Even with a rough start, the Pixel kept&nbsp;
growing, but it remained just a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.480
different from other Androids.
"Google tends to emphasise AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:14.000
capabilities over raw performance."
Not "better," but different.&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:26.080
But even if the Pixel is a bit different, why&nbsp;
do more budget-friendly phones seem to be dying?

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:30.640
Phone saturation is higher than it's ever&nbsp;
been, and the market is going through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.480
bit of a change. I think there are&nbsp;
four factors pressing the market.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:38.560
For ages, all kinds of brands around the&nbsp;
mid-tier or budget tier were offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.560
near-flagship specs, but cheaper.
"Good enough" smartphones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:47.680
like OnePlus, which is now struggling.
It saw shipments fall 20% in 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:52.320
is losing market share, and is even&nbsp;
closing down some of its US operations.&nbsp;

00:10:52.320 --> 00:10:58.880
The growth era of phones is over. Even if&nbsp;
the US is the most saturated, many countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.880 --> 00:11:03.280
are also reaching that saturation point.
Customers have already bought their first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:08.080
phone — often a cheaper one — and now&nbsp;
much of the growth comes from upgrading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:14.000
or stealing customers from other brands.
Exactly what Google and Apple are doing right now.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.480
They're offering features&nbsp;
that attract users looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:20.400
to move up. But it's not just "AI smartphone."&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.280
Research is showing that AI features&nbsp;
make people upgrade, but not switch.

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:30.240
I think what's happening is that more&nbsp;
people are moving up the smartphone ladder.&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:35.520
As phones last longer, they want smartphones with&nbsp;
more advanced features that will last them longer.

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:38.160
And that's what's really interesting.&nbsp;

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.840
The Pixel isn't really the most&nbsp;
powerful phone on the market.&nbsp;

00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:46.880
But it is premium, has that nice premium&nbsp;
experience, and it does what so many other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:51.760
phones can't — because of Google Tensor.
And to make matters worse, cheaper phones&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:55.760
are getting more expensive, thanks to&nbsp;
components like memory getting squeezed.&nbsp;

00:11:55.760 --> 00:12:01.280
Carl Pei, founder of OnePlus, then Nothing,&nbsp;
even wrote a long piece on exactly this.&nbsp;

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:07.040
"For fifteen years, the smartphone industry relied&nbsp;
on a single, reliable assumption: components would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:12.080
inevitably get cheaper. While short-term&nbsp;
volatility existed, the long-term downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:18.080
trend in memory and display costs allowed for&nbsp;
annual spec bumps without price hikes. In 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:24.960
that model has finally broken, driven by a&nbsp;
sharp and unprecedented surge in memory costs.&nbsp;

00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:31.840
Brands now face a simple choice: raise prices&nbsp;
— by 30% or more in some cases — or downgrade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.840 --> 00:12:37.040
specs. The 'more specs for less money' model&nbsp;
that many value brands were built on is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:43.120
longer sustainable in 2026. As a result, some&nbsp;
markets, particularly entry and mid-tier segments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.520
are likely to shrink by 20% or more, and&nbsp;
brands that have historically dominated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:52.000
these segments will struggle."
I think he's exactly right.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:57.120
For ages, Android smartphones were sort&nbsp;
of defined mostly by specs and pricing.&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:01.760
Brands were built entirely around this,&nbsp;
offering similar Snapdragons with a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.760 --> 00:13:06.240
different perks, or a cheaper shell.
But they're slowly disappearing.&nbsp;

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:08.880
Some went all in on being different aesthetically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:13.920
like Nothing, which is surviving.
But Google went a different direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:18.400
Build an entirely different chip.
It took longer and cost way more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:23.360
but no one can really do the same as a Pixel.
You can't just order the same chip from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.360 --> 00:13:29.600
Qualcomm and offer the same thing for cheaper.
The market that rewarded specs alone is gone.&nbsp;

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:34.480
Now a lot of android users are either&nbsp;
going to iPhone, or to Google Pixel.&nbsp;

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:40.000
People are buying more premium phones that&nbsp;
last, but also offer something different.

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:43.120
One of the exceptions to&nbsp;
this is Nothing, by Carl Pei.&nbsp;

00:13:43.120 --> 00:13:48.560
They own a tiny part of the Android market, and&nbsp;
while they're growing, they almost destroyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:59.044
themselves when they annoyed all their customers.
